# HOST suites — assert the LIVE deployed layer; run post-deploy by deploy-live.sh, NEVER in the
# tree verdict (the deployed-layer bootstrap circle).
#
# FORMAT (frozen contract — LAND_PIPELINE_V2 §4.2.2): plain text, one `tests/<name>.bats` per line,
# `#` comments and blank lines ignored. Consumers:
#   scripts/postland-verify.sh  runs `tests/*.bats` MINUS these lines  ⇒ the TREE verdict
#   scripts/deploy-live.sh      runs exactly these, AFTER an advance   ⇒ the LIVE HOST verdict
# The partition is a SET DIFFERENCE, so it is total by construction and never hand-synced. A missing
# manifest means EMPTY: the verifier then runs everything (fail-closed toward MORE proof, never
# less). An unparseable line is ignored and logged rather than silently narrowing the corpus.
#
# WHY this file exists: these suites assert the state of ~/.claude — the DEPLOYED layer. The tree
# under verification is by definition ahead of what is deployed, so pre-deploy they can only fail;
# but the deploy waits on the verdict they are failing. That circle is why 22 postland stamps
# produced 0 greens and every deploy since has been manual. Post-deploy, against the layer they
# actually describe, they are exactly the right check — so they move, they do not get deleted.
#
# ADDING one: a suite that reds in the verifier and passes standalone against the live layer is a
# host suite. Normal attribution already names the file, so it moves here by a one-line land, and
# the next tree carries the new partition with it.
#
# SECOND admission criterion — WHOLE-TREE META-LINT WRAPPERS (tests/test-hermeticity-lint.bats and
# tests/test-walltime-lint.bats). These two are here for a different reason than the six above: not
# because they assert the live layer, but because a bats suite that wraps a WHOLE-TREE assertion
# cannot run mid-corpus. The hermeticity wrapper passes standalone and fails from inside a full run
# — clean-room corpora failed on exactly that suite and nothing else (2,085 ok / 1 not-ok and 2,242
# ok / 1 not-ok; backlogs b59eb997d035, b4e49b4b5014), which localises the 14-consecutive-red-stamp
# deadlock to one file. The walltime wrapper is admitted on the CLASS, not on its own mid-corpus
# proof: it carries the same shape (`@test "the real tree is CLEAN — the embedded ratchet matches
# HEAD"`), so it inherits the same wedge risk, and it is redundant DETECTION — the check it wraps
# is already enforced standalone. Enforcement is NOT lost by either move: postland-verify runs
# scripts/test-walltime-lint.sh and scripts/test-hermeticity-lint.sh STANDALONE and
# whole-tree-strict BEFORE the corpus, so a real violation is a deterministic named RED that
# outranks a cut. Post-deploy, run standalone against the live layer, both wrappers are meaningful
# again — the hermeticity one's symlink-selftest failure shape was a real finding.
#
# The distinction matters when reading a red: a violation reported by the pre-corpus lint is about
# THE TREE; the same violation reported by one of these wrappers mid-corpus was about the run.

tests/deploy-parity.bats
tests/desk-arm-live.bats
tests/desk-recycle-durable.bats
tests/lr-team-audit.bats
tests/session-continue.bats
tests/waiting-recycle.bats

# whole-tree meta-lint wrappers (see the SECOND admission criterion above) — the checks they wrap
# are enforced standalone and pre-corpus by postland-verify.sh; re-run here post-deploy against the
# live layer, which is the only place a whole-tree assertion has a stable subject.
tests/test-hermeticity-lint.bats
tests/test-walltime-lint.bats
